WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:06.880
CSI: Crime Scene Investigation was a cultural
phenomenon that redefined the TV crime procedural

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.960
for the 21st century.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:15.500
The show's first spinoff, CSI: Miami, resuscitated
star David Caruso's career and was still pulling

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:20.990
in viewers when it was abruptly cancelled
after 10 seasons in 2012.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:24.940
And there’s only one way to find out what
went wrong.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.580
"What are you gonna do?"

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:32.619
"I...am gonna get to the truth."

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:33.649
Ratings Massacre

00:00:33.649 --> 00:00:40.739
In the 2011-12 TV season, which would prove
to be its last year on the air, CSI: Miami

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:44.770
ranked No. 27 out of all shows on broadcast
TV.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:50.579
Now that's pretty good…but not so good for
CBS, the most-watched network.

00:00:50.579 --> 00:00:54.960
It also represented a steady decline for the
once-mighty crime drama.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:01.870
In its debut season, CSI: Miami ranked No.
11, and then spent four years in the top 10

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.960
before starting an annual ratings slide.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:10.840
Also not helping was its Sunday night timeslot:
despite a lead-in from The Good Wife, CSI:

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:16.679
Miami got killed in the ratings by NBC's Sunday
Night Football.

00:01:16.679 --> 00:01:17.810
Cashing Out

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:22.909
The longer a show stays on the air, the more
contracts for all cast and crew members have

00:01:22.909 --> 00:01:29.899
to be renegotiated and re-signed, and various
union laws require major pay raises for everyone

00:01:29.899 --> 00:01:30.899
involved.

00:01:30.899 --> 00:01:35.430
Sometimes, it's worth the production company
and network's money—The Big Bang Theory,

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:41.840
for example, has a main cast that earns $1
million per episode as it enters its tenth

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:42.840
season.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.720
But that's the exception to the rule.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:50.249
Generally, the longer a show airs, their ratings
decline—just like CSI: Miami.

00:01:50.249 --> 00:01:55.919
That means a network is paying more money
for a show fewer people watch, and it's just

00:01:55.919 --> 00:01:56.919
not cost-effective.

00:01:56.919 --> 00:01:58.070
"Verdict's already in."

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:02.880
"The verdict is in, Frank, but the jury is
out."

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.099
Fleeing The Scene

00:02:04.099 --> 00:02:09.649
Speaking of being cost effective, CBS has
a long history of keeping shows on the air

00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:14.079
because it's cheap to make them in pre-existing
production facilities.

00:02:14.079 --> 00:02:20.519
For example, in 1980, the Hawaii-set of Magnum,
P.I. made it to air in part because the network

00:02:20.519 --> 00:02:25.530
could use the studios and crew they'd had
in the area for the production of the original

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:29.400
Hawaii Five-O since the 1960s.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:35.660
In 2012, CBS had a string of New York-based
cop shows in production, including CSI: New

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:42.040
York, Blue Bloods, and NYC-22, and wanted
to present them all in a single three-hour

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.000
block on Friday nights.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.489
With those shows all produced in and around
New York, there wasn't room in either the

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:54.209
budget or the schedule for a show shot in
the far-off, expensive city of Miami—or

00:02:54.209 --> 00:02:55.209
worse.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.220
"We're going to Brazil."

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:00.439
Stiff Competition

00:03:00.439 --> 00:03:07.260
At the same time it canceled CSI: Miami, CBS
renewed the other CSI spinoff on the air at

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.049
the time, CSI: New York.

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:14.969
And CSI: Miami had better overall ratings
than its Gotham-based counterpart.

00:03:14.969 --> 00:03:16.189
What gives?

00:03:16.189 --> 00:03:23.230
Well, CSI: New York had helped turn the little-watched
TV wasteland of Friday nights into a big draw

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:24.439
for CBS.

00:03:24.439 --> 00:03:30.480
It brought in fewer viewers than CSI: Miami,
but for Friday night it trailed only its lead-out,

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.349
Blue Bloods, in the ratings.

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:39.859
So CSI: New York lived because it was a big
fish in a smaller pond, while CSI: Miami got

00:03:39.859 --> 00:03:40.859
lost in the shuffle.

00:03:40.859 --> 00:03:48.549
"We have a victim that started the weekend
big man on campus, and ended it dead on arrival."

00:03:48.549 --> 00:03:49.629
Thanks for watching!

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:53.910
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:58.280
And leave us a comment to let us know which
CSI series was your favorite."

